The hardest part of software engineering, especially language design, is *keeping complexity down.*


It's one of those things that's easy to say, but there's only a few languages out there that really live up to this.


# Complexity is complex

Let's not over-simplify it! There are plenty of ways a language can be complex:

 * A lack of rules, which means we have to hold a lot more in our heads (C, Javascript).
 * A few simple rules that lead to a lot of *emergent complexity* (Rust, Haskell, etc.)
 * Many small rules that *interact in complex ways* (C++).


But before we analyze these differences, let's first talk about *inherent* versus *incidental* complexity.



# Inherent vs incidental complexity

Remember that piece of code you wrote, that you later simplified?

That "removable" complexity is *incidental complexity.*

In fact, there's probably even more ways you could go back and simplify that code. There's more incidental complexity, even if you can't see it yet.


However, at some point, you can't simplify any more. The code is still pretty intricate though. It has to be, it was a hard problem you were solving!

The remaining un-removable complexity is *inherent complexity.* It will always be there, no matter how much you simplify.


# Example: Static Typing

Static typing is pretty complex. There's structs, and interfaces, and generics, and a lot of concepts that we have to learn.


But is it inherently or artificially complex?


Well, even in dynamically-typed languages like Javascript and Python, we still think about an object's type even if we don't inform the language about it.

<<<<
Even in this dynamically-typed Javascript function, we know that `ship` should have two members: an `hp` integer and a `name` string.
////
```js
function printSpaceship(ship) {
  console.log(ship.hp / 100 + "%");
  console.log(ship.name);
}
```
>>>>


# Example: `async`/`await`

In a web server, when we receive a request we usually spun up a thread to handle it.

Of course, a thread takes a lot of memory. So many languages added `async`/`await`, which uses a lot less memory to represent an ongoing operation.


There was a downside, of course. `async`/`await` makes our code more complex, as illustrated by Bob Nystrom's [What Color Is Your Function?](https://journal.stuffwithstuff.com/2015/02/01/what-color-is-your-function/) article.


But there's no other way to get anything that small. `async`/`await`'s complexity is unremovable if you need to scale a server. It's inherent complexity.


...or is it?


# Inherent complexity is a tempting lie we tell ourselves

So let's take a step back, and realize that we humans are _terrible_ at identifying inherent complexity.

We often look at some complexity and think "that's unavoidable," and declare it as Inherent Complexity™ and move on with our lives.


This was the case with `async`/`await`, as [goroutines](https://dev.to/ankitmalikg/what-is-goroutine-1j6d) and Java's [Project Loom](https://www.baeldung.com/openjdk-project-loom) showed us (and there's even ways to do Loom-like concurrency without garbage collection).


This brings us to an interesting truth: inherent complexity can be reduced.


"But then maybe it wasn't inherent complexity to begin with!"

Technically true. However, if we can't identify inherent complexity, then perhaps it's time we retire it as a concept.


# How do we reduce inherent complexity?

We move things to bette


We need to be wary of global complexity costs to tiny problems.












However, that's often not the case. For example:

 * We thought that `async`/`await` was inherent complexity, because there was no possible way to make a server ridiculously scalable without it. 
 * We thought that borrow checking's complexity was inherent because it was the only way to get really fast memory-safe code, but then [generational references and regions](https://verdagon.dev/blog/generational-references) proved us wrong.
 * (need one more example, two more preferably because dont wanna talk about gen refs and regions)


In all these cases, we reduced this "inherent" complexity by abstracting it away.


In other words *good abstractions reduce complexity.*


So how do we use this in languages?


# Abstraction










I'd say that *static typing is inherent complexity* in general. Of course, when you consider languages like C++ where their type system is [literally turing complete](https://stackoverflow.com/a/275295/1424454) then you get into incidental complexity territory.


In fact, when a language doesn't help us keep the types straight, we have to do it ourselves. We have to think harder to make sure our program is correct.

You can really feel this when refactoring. When you're refactoring a statically typed language, you can sometimes mindlessly just follow the compiler errors until the refactor is done. With dynamically typed languages, you need to think a lot harder.


However, this isn't a black-and-white judgment.

 * If you're writing something small that only deals with simple types, it's simple enough to fit in your head.
 * If it's a program that runs quickly and has pretty simple inputs, there's not much difference between detecting it at compile-time or at run-time.






I've been dealing a lot with the Rust/Vale FFI boundary a lot lately, so I'll use some examples from Rust that I used to believe.

 * I thought `async`/`await` is inherent complexity, because we need to be aware of where our thread might be interrupted.
 * I thought borrow checking is inherent complexity, because we need to avoid data races and even [single-threaded race conditions](https://manishearth.github.io/blog/2015/05/17/the-problem-with-shared-mutability/).
 * I thought `Result<T, E>` is inherent complexity, because we need to consider errors that happen in a function we're calling.


"Of course!" says the computer scientist, "we need to be absolutely rigorous about these things! We need to prevent these things."


"Not so fast," says the software engineer. "We have to balance these against other real-world concerns."












Thinking these thoughts makes the computer scientist in me happy. However, the engineer in me knows better.


You see, those three things


However, all of these come with a complexity cost.


