# Something can be simple and complex









Have you ever heard someone say "No, no, it's simple! Here..." and then explain something that's actually very complex?

By the end of their explanation, you're left wondering, "How can they think that's simple? I only barely grasp what they're getting at."


The explainer has something called *the curse of knowledge.*

I am very familiar with software-related curses, after what happened in Campeche with [a certain ancient tome](/grimoire/grimoire#a-cursd-tome-and-ancient-memory-safety).

Overcoming this curse is one of the most important skills for a language designer, a software architect, or anyone who wants to communicate well in general.


# Macs are simple

A few years ago, my friend bought a Mac.

He'd only ever used Windows before, but Macs are pretty simple, so I figured he'd be able to pick it up pretty quickly. He was a computer science major, after all.

I started walking him through the Mac basics, and I very quickly realized that *Macs are not simple.*


They're actually _really_ unintuitive:

 * An app can have multiple windows. The menu bar is associated with the current window, but the dock icon is associated with the whole program.
 * There's no start bar, or central entry point of any kind. You just gotta know how to get to things.
 * To install an application, you drag it to the applications folder. A lot of applications just show you two icons and an arrow and expect you to know what to do.


After that, I thought, "Why in the world did I think Mac OS was simple?"


And more importantly, what else did I mistakenly think was simple?


# It's not simple, you're just used to it

I see this happening all the time.


People say C is simple, because it's really just a mapping to assembly, which is pretty simple.

People say Haskell is simple, because it's just like regular programming but where everything is immutable.

People say the borrow checker is simple, because it's really just one rule: no shared mutability.


Brent Yorgey [famously wrote about](https://byorgey.wordpress.com/2009/01/12/abstraction-intuition-and-the-monad-tutorial-fallacy/) how people get this so wrong when trying to explain the simple concept of monads:

> To see what I mean, imagine the following scenario: Joe Haskeller is trying to learn about monads. After struggling to understand them for a week, looking at examples, writing code, reading things other people have written, he finally has an “aha!” moment: everything is suddenly clear, and Joe Understands Monads! What has really happened, of course, is that Joe’s brain has fit all the details together into a higher-level abstraction, a metaphor which Joe can use to get an intuitive grasp of monads; let us suppose that Joe’s metaphor is that Monads are Like Burritos. Here is where Joe badly misinterprets his own thought process: “Of course!” Joe thinks. “It’s all so simple now. The key to understanding monads is that they are Like Burritos. If only I had thought of this before!” The problem, of course, is that if Joe HAD thought of this before, it wouldn’t have helped: the week of struggling through details was a necessary and integral part of forming Joe’s Burrito intuition, not a sad consequence of his failure to hit upon the idea sooner. 


The effect gets worse with time.

It's been so long since I've learned multiplication, that I've completely forgotten how hard it was. Then I hear my small relatives say "multiplication table" and it all comes back... we had to memorize so much, just to multiply numbers!


